---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Callout from "@dst/ui/Callout.astro";
---
<Layout
  title="How to Start a Business in Dubai"
  description="A practical guide to starting a business in Dubai: choosing between mainland, free zone, and offshore structures, what registration actually costs, and what to sort out afterwards."
>
  <section class="container hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Company formation</p>
    <h1>How to start a business in Dubai</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Dubai remains one of the most straightforward places in the world to
      register a company as a foreign founder — no local sponsor requirement
      in most free zones, full profit repatriation, and a licensing process
      that in many cases takes days rather than months. The harder question
      isn't whether you can register a company here; it's which structure
      actually fits what you're building.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Three structures, one decision</h2>
    <p>
      Every company in Dubai falls into one of three categories, and the
      choice between them shapes almost everything downstream — what you can
      legally do, where you can do it, and what banking and visa options open
      up afterwards.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Structure</th><th>Best for</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Free zone</strong></td>
            <td>Freelancers, consultancies, e-commerce, most service and tech businesses</td>
            <td>Cannot trade directly in the UAE mainland market without a distributor or extra licensing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Mainland</strong></td>
            <td>Businesses that need to sell directly to UAE-based customers or bid on government contracts</td>
            <td>More paperwork up front; office requirements are stricter</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Offshore</strong></td>
            <td>Holding structures, asset ownership, international trading with no UAE presence needed</td>
            <td>Cannot obtain UAE residency visas or operate locally</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Most first-time founders reading this are choosing between free zone
      and mainland. See the <a href="/free-zones/">free zone comparison</a>
      and the <a href="/mainland/">mainland guide</a> for the detail each one
      deserves rather than a flattened summary here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>What it actually costs</h2>
    <p>
      Free zone company formation typically starts in the low five figures
      (AED), scaling with the number of visas and business activities
      attached to the licence. Mainland setup costs cover a similar range but
      often carry additional office-lease and approval fees depending on the
      activity. Treat any number you read online — including here — as a
      starting estimate, not a quote: fees change, and they vary by free
      zone, activity, and visa count. Confirm current pricing directly with
      your chosen free zone authority or DED before committing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>After registration</h2>
    <p>
      Getting the licence is the beginning, not the end. Three things
      founders consistently underestimate:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/banking/">Opening a business bank account</a></strong> —
        often the slowest step in the whole process, and one banks approach
        very differently depending on your nationality, business activity,
        and expected transaction volume.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/taxation/">Corporate tax and filing</a></strong> —
        the UAE's corporate tax regime is recent and does apply to most
        companies above the small-business threshold; assuming "no tax"
        because of Dubai's reputation is a genuinely costly mistake.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/payment-processing/">Accepting payments online</a></strong> —
        a specific, common question for software and services businesses,
        answered plainly on its own page rather than buried in a general FAQ.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Callout>
    Building a software product or working as an independent developer?
    The <a href="/payment-processing/">payment processing page</a> answers
    the question most guides skip: what a Dubai company actually needs to
    accept card payments through a processor like Stripe.
  </Callout>
</Layout>

